' SLEEP waits against a deadline rather than counting slices. Counting
' assumed a five-millisecond slice took five milliseconds; on Windows the
' default timer granularity is about 15.6 ms, so every slice took three
' times its length and SLEEP 1000 waited 3.2 seconds.
'
' The bounds here are deliberately loose. A sleep can only ever be late,
' never early, and how late depends on the platform's timer: one tick of
' it per slice, which is 15.6 ms on Windows and under a millisecond on
' Linux. What the upper bounds catch is the old behaviour, which was a
' multiple rather than an offset.

DIM fails = 0

SUB CHK(what$, got, want)
    IF got <> want THEN
        PRINT "FAIL: "; what$; " = "; got; ", expected "; want
        fails = fails + 1
    ENDIF
ENDSUB

DIM t0, took

' 500 ms took about 1600 before the fix.
t0 = TICK()
SLEEP 500
took = TICK() - t0
CHK "SLEEP 500 is not early", took >= 450, TRUE
CHK "SLEEP 500 is not a multiple", took < 1200, TRUE

' 1000 took about 3200.
t0 = TICK()
SLEEP 1000
took = TICK() - t0
CHK "SLEEP 1000 is not early", took >= 950, TRUE
CHK "SLEEP 1000 is not a multiple", took < 2000, TRUE

' Zero and negative must return at once rather than loop.
t0 = TICK()
SLEEP 0
CHK "SLEEP 0 returns", TICK() - t0 < 100, TRUE

t0 = TICK()
SLEEP -5
CHK "SLEEP with a negative returns", TICK() - t0 < 100, TRUE

' A run of short sleeps stays proportional. Each one still costs at
' least one timer tick, so the total is bounded from below by the count
' rather than by the sum, which is why the ceiling is generous.
t0 = TICK()
DIM i
FOR i = 1 TO 20
    SLEEP 10
NEXT i
took = TICK() - t0
CHK "20 short sleeps are not early", took >= 180, TRUE
CHK "20 short sleeps do not multiply", took < 1200, TRUE

IF fails = 0 THEN
    PRINT "ALL TESTS PASSED!"
ELSE
    PRINT "RESULTS: "; fails; " failed"
ENDIF
